1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,475
This is the lecture the course description warned you about, and promised you

2
00:00:04,475 --> 00:00:06,457
wouldn't need to be a programmer for.

3
00:00:06,457 --> 00:00:07,796
Here's that promise kept.

4
00:00:07,796 --> 00:00:09,939
The spreadsheet from last lecture works.

5
00:00:09,939 --> 00:00:11,064
It will keep working.

6
00:00:11,064 --> 00:00:15,349
This version exists for one reason: it can run itself, automatically, every time

7
00:00:15,349 --> 00:00:19,099
someone proposes a change, without anybody remembering to open a file.

8
00:00:19,099 --> 00:00:23,331
Eighteen lines, and we're going to read every single one before this lecture is

9
00:00:23,331 --> 00:00:23,599
over.

10
00:00:23,599 --> 00:00:26,384
If you've never written a line of code, that's fine.

11
00:00:26,384 --> 00:00:29,009
Ever followed a recipe you didn't write yourself?

12
00:00:29,009 --> 00:00:31,045
This isn't more complicated than that.

13
00:00:31,045 --> 00:00:33,241
Nothing to install, nothing to configure.

14
00:00:33,241 --> 00:00:36,937
Python already understands spreadsheets and counting, out of the box.

15
00:00:36,937 --> 00:00:40,580
You're not learning a programming language in the next nine minutes.

16
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:45,080
You're reading eighteen lines somebody else already wrote, closely enough to explain

17
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:45,615
them back.

18
00:00:45,615 --> 00:00:49,901
And if you never run it yourself, that's a completely reasonable outcome of this

19
00:00:49,901 --> 00:00:50,329
lecture.

20
00:00:50,329 --> 00:00:54,615
The goal is that you can look at what an engineer built for you and know whether

21
00:00:54,615 --> 00:00:58,365
it's doing what you asked, not that you become the one maintaining it.

22
00:00:58,915 --> 00:01:03,603
There's the first half, and notice something before we go further: nothing on this

23
00:01:03,603 --> 00:01:05,433
slide is longer than a sentence.

24
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:09,378
Lines one and two borrow two tools instead of building them yourself.

25
00:01:09,378 --> 00:01:12,752
One reads a file shaped like a spreadsheet, comma by comma.

26
00:01:12,752 --> 00:01:17,383
The other lets you count things by bucket without listing the buckets in advance.

27
00:01:17,383 --> 00:01:22,129
Lines four to six set up two empty counters, one for totals and one for passes, and

28
00:01:22,129 --> 00:01:26,760
name your six rubric columns once so the rest of the script can just say "rubric"

29
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:28,818
instead of repeating them six times.

30
00:01:28,818 --> 00:01:29,619
Then the loop.

31
00:01:29,619 --> 00:01:32,821
Open the file, and read it one row, one case, at a time.

32
00:01:32,821 --> 00:01:37,567
For each row: read its bucket, read its six scores, add one to that bucket's total,

33
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:41,683
and if all six scores say pass, add one to that bucket's pass count too.

34
00:01:41,683 --> 00:01:45,857
That's the only real judgement in the whole script, repeated fifty times.

35
00:01:46,407 --> 00:01:50,949
Walk case C027 from last lecture through the last block on the previous slide.

36
00:01:50,949 --> 00:01:52,404
Bucket reads adversarial.

37
00:01:52,404 --> 00:01:54,850
Scores read one, one, zero, one, one, one.

38
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:59,217
Sum that list and you get five, not six, so the if is false, and passes for

39
00:01:59,217 --> 00:02:00,789
adversarial does not go up.

40
00:02:00,789 --> 00:02:02,768
Totals for adversarial still does.

41
00:02:02,768 --> 00:02:07,601
Now the three lines above: for every bucket seen, divide passes by total, and print

42
00:02:07,601 --> 00:02:09,290
it, lined up in neat columns.

43
00:02:09,290 --> 00:02:13,889
Here's what actually prints, using the same numbers from the dashboard lecture.

44
00:02:13,889 --> 00:02:18,547
Adversarial at forty percent, the collapse you already know about, sitting right

45
00:02:18,547 --> 00:02:19,712
there in plain text.

46
00:02:19,712 --> 00:02:24,370
No spreadsheet required to see it, and no formula to click on to double-check it

47
00:02:24,370 --> 00:02:24,777
either.

48
00:02:24,777 --> 00:02:25,825
It's just printed.

49
00:02:26,375 --> 00:02:30,977
Here's the actual reason this version exists, and it isn't "code is better." Wire

50
00:02:30,977 --> 00:02:35,636
this script into wherever your engineers already store the prompt or the code, and

51
00:02:35,636 --> 00:02:39,500
it runs itself, automatically, every time someone proposes a change.

52
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:40,806
Nobody has to remember.

53
00:02:40,806 --> 00:02:43,818
Nobody has to open a spreadsheet at nine on a Friday.

54
00:02:43,818 --> 00:02:48,249
If a bucket falls below whatever threshold you've set, the change gets blocked

55
00:02:48,249 --> 00:02:50,181
before it ships, not caught after.

56
00:02:50,181 --> 00:02:54,612
If every bucket holds, it merges, and nobody had to be in the room for that to

57
00:02:54,612 --> 00:02:55,578
happen correctly.

58
00:02:55,578 --> 00:03:00,351
That's the entire pitch for these eighteen lines over the version you already built.

59
00:03:00,351 --> 00:03:01,032
Not smarter.

60
00:03:01,032 --> 00:03:02,680
Just present when you aren't.

61
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:07,396
This is also exactly what "regression testing" meant, back when the term was just a

62
00:03:07,396 --> 00:03:07,850
diagram.

63
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,271
The diagram was the idea.

64
00:03:09,271 --> 00:03:13,986
This script, wired into that diagram, is the idea actually running, on every single

65
00:03:13,986 --> 00:03:18,475
proposed change, including the ones proposed at ten at night by someone who has

66
00:03:18,475 --> 00:03:19,270
never met you.

67
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:20,984
Say the cost plainly.

68
00:03:20,984 --> 00:03:23,366
This script has never scored a single case.

69
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:27,966
It only reads scores that already exist, from your rubric and your validated judge,

70
00:03:27,966 --> 00:03:29,794
exactly like the spreadsheet did.

71
00:03:29,794 --> 00:03:33,950
Nothing about switching to code makes the judgement part faster or cheaper.

72
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:38,383
And reading eighteen lines is genuinely different from wiring them into wherever

73
00:03:38,383 --> 00:03:39,879
your company runs its code.

74
00:03:39,879 --> 00:03:44,423
That step, connecting this script to actually block a bad change automatically, is

75
00:03:44,423 --> 00:03:48,745
worth five minutes of an engineer's time, not an argument about whether you're

76
00:03:48,745 --> 00:03:49,964
allowed to ask for it.

77
00:03:49,964 --> 00:03:51,183
You've read the logic.

78
00:03:51,183 --> 00:03:54,065
Let someone who does this daily handle the plumbing.

79
00:03:54,065 --> 00:03:57,556
One more honest limit: this script trusts its input completely.

80
00:03:57,556 --> 00:04:02,155
Feed it a CSV where a score column holds "yes" instead of one or zero, and it won't

81
00:04:02,155 --> 00:04:05,480
warn you, it will simply crash, or worse, silently miscount.

82
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,583
A spreadsheet at least shows you the wrong-looking cell.

83
00:04:08,583 --> 00:04:11,243
Code shows you a stack trace, or nothing at all.

84
00:04:11,793 --> 00:04:14,772
Notice this definition still isn't about programming.

85
00:04:14,772 --> 00:04:19,045
It's about who has to be in the room for the check to happen: a human with a

86
00:04:19,045 --> 00:04:21,069
spreadsheet open, or nothing at all.

87
00:04:21,069 --> 00:04:25,567
The exercise is deliberately the smallest possible test: export what you already

88
00:04:25,567 --> 00:04:29,109
built, run this against it, and confirm the two versions agree.

89
00:04:29,109 --> 00:04:33,550
If they don't, before you suspect anything clever, check that your CSV's column

90
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:36,080
headers say exactly q1 through q6 and bucket.

91
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:39,735
That single mismatch causes nearly every disagreement you'll see.

92
00:04:39,735 --> 00:04:44,401
Two independent implementations of the same arithmetic agreeing is not a formality.

93
00:04:44,401 --> 00:04:48,786
It's the same principle as the calibration lecture, applied to code instead of

94
00:04:48,786 --> 00:04:53,284
people: two workings, checked against each other, before either one gets trusted

95
00:04:53,284 --> 00:04:53,621
alone.

96
00:04:54,171 --> 00:04:55,839
One thing before you move on.

97
00:04:55,839 --> 00:04:59,463
The script, exactly as shown, is A04, attached to this lecture.

98
00:04:59,463 --> 00:05:04,294
Copy it, don't retype it, so a typo doesn't become your first debugging session, and

99
00:05:04,294 --> 00:05:06,595
run it against your own exported scores.

100
00:05:06,595 --> 00:05:11,426
Confirm it agrees with the spreadsheet before you trust either one for a real launch

101
00:05:11,426 --> 00:05:11,944
decision.

102
00:05:11,944 --> 00:05:16,717
Next lecture, we answer the question this section has been quietly avoiding: when a

103
00:05:16,717 --> 00:05:21,549
pass rate moves, how do you actually know whether that's real, or the ordinary noise

104
00:05:21,549 --> 00:05:22,987
of re-running fifty dice.
